% TRIO, middle part, bars 185-208 (c003: 24 bars; v03 bars 189-196 cut).
% c085: 193 Vla "unis." after the div. thirds of 169-184 (Adler PDF 30). 192 and 208 Cb rest: 5 beats for pizz.->arco at 193 and 209 (Adler PDF 54; harp F sharp 1 at 192, Vc B2 at 208 keep the bass).
% c035: 185 Ob 1 prints "1."; 193-196 Bn 1 solo (\partCombineSoloI: one voice with "1." instead of Bn 2's bar rests; likewise 205-208) (no label was reprinted after the shared rest).
% c022: 200 Ob 2 D sharp, Cl 1 B sharp: no fifths/octaves from iv to V; 203 viola D sharp: no fifths E-A/F sharp-B with the bass (A-S PDF 189-190).
% 185-192: call and response: Ob 1 in C sharp minor (185); the cellos, doubled an octave up by the cor anglais, answer a step lower, in B major (189).
%
% 193-200: the strings together, crescendo to the trio's forte: the oboe's call in C sharp minor in Vln I and II, ending on G sharp (V of C sharp).
% 201-208: A major, then A minor (mixture, iv), to the dominant pedal B; T's head in Cl 1 and Fl 1, diminuendo; the 7th A on the last beat (V7) falls to G sharp at 209.

% c042: 192-193 Cl 1 takes the harp's A sharp 4 (pp) and moves to A itself, so F sharp major -> F sharp minor is a chromatic step in one voice (A-S PDF 394, Ex. 23-12b).
% c032: 196-197 V7-vi: the 7th A only in Cl 1 (violas B, not A), Cl 1 A -> G# and Cl 2 D# -> E (the clarinet parts 197-200 exchanged an octave), so the 7th falls
%   and the leading tone rises into the deceptive C sharp minor (A-S PDF 76, 202).
% c033: 207-208 crescendo in the B-pedal parts, the timpani roll and the motto-tail line (Fl 1, Cl 1), so T's forte return at 209 is reached, not dropped in
%   after pp (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 127, amplification: the second group enters soft and crescendos faster; PDF 130, crescendo strings - wood - brass).
% c034: 197-200 Fl 1 doubles Vln I's E6 call at the trio's forte (Adler II PDF 151-152: the flute alone carries the top octave; Mozart 40/iv, Beethoven 9/i).
% c047: 197 Hn 1-2 mf, a degree under the woodwinds' f: Hn 1 doubles Cl 1's T at the unison and would cover it (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108: in forte the horns
%   overwhelm the reeds; PDF 112: mark the woodwind one degree louder than the brass).
% c039: 197-200 no A4 against a held G sharp 4 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 132-133: a florid line and its harmonic basis in one register clash; keep them an octave apart):
%   Hn 1 moves with T (G sharp-A, B, A tied over as a 9-8 suspension into 200), Ob 2 E4 at 197, Ob 1 and Vln II end the call A4-G sharp 4 with T.
% c037: 197-200 Cl 1 and the violas play T's first phrase at its own pitch (G sharp-A-B-E, D sharp-C sharp-B, A-G sharp) against the oboe's call
%   in Fl 1, Ob 1 and the violins, instead of holding G sharp and C sharp: the trio's two ideas combined at its forte, over C sharp minor - F sharp minor - G sharp
%   (Adler II PDF 230-231: Schoenberg's countertheme of the bar's pitches in its own rhythm; PDF 203: layers of linear activity sounding together).
% c061: 195-196 Vln I, Vln II (and Bsn 1) tie G sharp over the barline into the V7 (bass B, Cl 1 A) and resolve to F sharp on beat 2: the 19th-century
%   7/6-5 suspension over V7 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 353-354 sec. 15, Ex. 21-15e-f, Josef Strauss), so the string build no longer repeats T's
%   rhythm 4.-8-4 | 2. (c021) and the crescendo into 197 peaks on a dissonance (PDF 348: suspensions bring a line into prominence).
% c091: 185, 189, 197 the call's first bar is a real five-note turn (main tone, upper neighbour, main tone, lower neighbour, main tone: E-F#-E-D#-E;
%   answer D#-E-D#-C#-D#) instead of E-D#-C#-D#-E, a lower passing excursion that has no upper neighbour and anticipates the C# of the next bar
%   (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 338-339, Ex. 20-21b Mahler: the nineteenth-century turn; PDF 335: the neighbour figure treated as a motive).
tBFluteOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e'''4(\f fis'''8 e'''8 dis'''8 e'''8 | cis'''2 gis''4) | a''4.( b''8 cis'''4) | gis''2.\> | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineSoloII R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineAutomatic }  % c036: 205-208 go to Fl 2, so that Fl 1 enters fresh with T at 209 (it had 20 bars, 205-224, with no rest; Adler PDF 188, Brahms 1/iv: the second flute takes over so the first can breathe; PDF 192: multiple flutes relieve one another)
% c084: 197-200 Fl 2 doubles Fl 1's call a2 at E6 (was C sharp 5-B sharp 4 held f, the flute's weak low register inside oboes, clarinets, horns and violas;
%   Casanelles PDF 275: the flute's weak fundamental in its low register; PDF 287: sounds in one band mask each other). B sharp stays in Cl 2 and Bsn 1.
tBFluteTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e'''4(\f fis'''8 e'''8 dis'''8 e'''8 | cis'''2 gis''4) | a''4.( b''8 cis'''4) | gis''2.\> | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | gis''2(\pp a''4 | b''2.~ | b''2\< ais''4 | a''2.) | }
% c023: 206-209 Fl 1 and Cl 1 hold B, then A sharp (207, beat 3) and A (208, the V7's 7th) to G sharp at 209: the motto's tail B-A sharp-A-G sharp,
%   at the pitch of Hn 1's close (coda), leads into T's return (A&S PDF 600-602: Mozart K. 542 quotes and enlarges the same B-A sharp-A-G sharp).
% c021: the calls at 185, 189 and 197 open with a quaver turn, so the trio's middle part does not repeat T's rhythm (2-4 | 2-4 | 4.-8-4 | 2.) a fifth to eighth time
tBOboeOne = { e''4(\p^\markup { \bold "1." \italic "espress." } fis''8 e''8 dis''8 e''8 | cis''2 gis'4) | a'4.( b'8 cis''4) | gis'2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e''4(\f fis''8 e''8 dis''8 e''8 | cis''2 gis'4) | a'4.( b'8 cis''4) | a'4(\> gis'2) | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c094: 197-200 Ob 2 rests (it held E4-G sharp 4-F sharp 4-D sharp 4 in its low register under Cl 1's T, in unison with Hn 2, and in 2nds, 7ths, 4ths and 5ths with Ob 1's call;
%   Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 90 rules 2-4: normal order of register, matching registers, concords to two instruments of one colour, 'specially' the oboe). The notes stay in Hn 2 and Cl 2.
tBOboeTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
tBCorAnglais = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | dis'4(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" e'8 dis'8 cis'8 dis'8 | b2 fis4) | gis4.( a8 b4) | fis2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
tBClOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | ais'2.(\pp | a'2.)\p\< | cis''2. | b'2. | a'2. | gis'2(\f a'4 | b'2 e''4 | dis''4. cis''8 b'4 | a'4\> gis'2) | \partCombineSoloII R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineAutomatic }  % c036: 205-208 to Cl 2 (the D sharp stays in Hn 3), so Cl 1 rests 201-208 before T at 209 (Adler PDF 188, 192)
tBClTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | fis'2.\p\< | fis'2 e'4~ | e'2. | dis'2. | e'2.\f | e'2. | cis'2. | bis2.\> | e'2.\p | e'2. | dis'2.\> | dis'2. | gis'2(\p a'4 | b'2.~ | b'2\< ais'4 | a'2.) | }
tBBsnOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineSoloI a2.\p\< | a2. | gis2.~ | gis4( fis4) r4 | \partCombineAutomatic gis2.\f | gis2. | fis2. | dis'2.\> | cis'2.\p | c'2. | b2.\> | a2. | \partCombineSoloI fis2.\! | fis2. | fis2. | fis2 r4 | }  % c066: Bsn 1 breathes before T's return at 209 (held notes 197-216 otherwise, about 16 s; Adler PDF 176, 292)
tBBsnTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineSoloI R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineAutomatic cis2.\f | cis2. | fis,2. | gis,2.\> | a,2.\p | a,2. | b,2.\> | b,2. | \partCombineSoloI R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | }  % c075: Bsn 2 shares Bsn 1's diminuendo at 203-204 (the shared staff prints one hairpin; Bsn 2's own part lacked it; MOLA PDF 8)
% c088: 197-200 Hn 1-2 slurred like the Cl 1/violas phrase they join; 201-208 Hn 3-4 portato slurs (Adler I PDF 309: one slur, one breath; soft tonguing).
tBHnOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | gis'2(\mf a'4 | b'2. | a'2.~ | a'4\> gis'2) | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
tBHnTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e'2.--(\mf | e'2.-- | fis'2.-- | dis'2.--\>) | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
tBHnThree = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e'2.--(\pp | e'2.-- | dis'2.--\> | dis'2.--) | dis'2.--(\! | dis'2.-- | dis'2.--\< | dis'2.--) | }
tBHnFour = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | cis'2.--(\pp | c'2.-- | b2.--\> | b2.--) | b2.--(\! | b2.-- | b2.--\< | b2.--) | }
tBTimp = { R2. | R2. | R2. | gis,4\pp r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | gis,4\f r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | b,2.:32\pp~ | b,2.:32~ | b,2.:32\<~ | b,4\mf r4 r4 | }
% c027: 189-192 harp chords an octave up (B4-F sharp 5) above the cellos' and cor anglais' answer (F sharp 3-D sharp 4), which they covered and crossed (Adler II PDF 257, 274)
tBHarpR = { r4 <gis cis' e'>4 <gis cis' e'>4 | r4 <gis cis' e'>4 <gis cis' e'>4 | r4 <a cis' fis'>4 <a cis' fis'>4 | r4_\markup \small "C♮" <gis c' dis'>4 <gis c' dis'>4 | r4 <b' dis'' fis''>4 <b' dis'' fis''>4 | r4 <b' dis'' fis''>4_\markup \small "C♯ A♯" <b' dis'' fis''>4 | r4 <gis' b' e''>4 <gis' b' e''>4 | r4 <ais' cis'' fis''>4 <ais' cis'' fis''>4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c099: harp pp (was p, c094) under Ob 1's p espress. call and the cor anglais/cello answer (Casanelles PDF 271-272).
tBHarpL = { cis,4\pp r4 r4 | cis,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | gis,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
tBVlnOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | fis''2(\p\< gis''4 | a''2 cis'''4) | b''4.( a''8 gis''4~ | gis''4 fis''2) | e'''4(\f fis'''8 e'''8 dis'''8 e'''8 | cis'''2 gis''4) | a''4.( b''8 cis'''4) | gis''2.\> | a'4--\p r4 r4 | c''4-- r4 r4 | b'4-- r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }  % c070: 203 the top voice's flat 6 (C5, A minor at 202) falls to 5 (B4) over the B pedal in its own register, with Bsn 1 and Hn 4 an octave lower (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 23, PDF 388 and PDF 401 point 3: once introduced, flat 6 moves to 5)
tBVlnTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | fis'2(\p\< gis'4 | a'2 cis''4) | b'4.( a'8 gis'4~ | gis'4 fis'2) | e''4(\f fis''8 e''8 dis''8 e''8 | cis''2 gis'4) | a'4.( b'8 cis''4) | a'4(\> gis'2) | cis'4--\p r4 r4 | e'4-- r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
tBVla = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | a2.\p\<^\markup \italic "unis." | a2. | gis2. | b2. | gis'2(\f_\markup \italic "espress." a'4 | b'2 e''4) | dis''4.( cis''8 b'4) | a'4(\> gis'2) | e'2.\p | e'2. | dis'2.\> | fis'2. | fis'2.\! | fis'2. | fis'2.\< | fis'2. | }
tBVc = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | dis'4(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" e'8 dis'8 cis'8 dis'8 | b2 fis4) | gis4.( a8 b4) | fis2. | fis,2.\p\< | fis,2. | gis,2. | b,2. | cis2.\f | cis2. | fis,2. | gis,2.\> | a,2.\p | a,2. | b,2. | b,2. | b,2. | b,2. | b,2.\< | b,2. | }  % c011: the answer at 189-192 an octave higher, on the A and D strings, in unison with the cor anglais (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 56-57)
tBCb = { cis,4 r4 r4 | cis,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | gis,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | R2. | fis,,2.\p\<^\markup \italic "arco" | fis,,2. | gis,,2. | b,,2. | cis,2.\f | cis,2. | fis,,2. | gis,,2.\> | a,,2.\p | a,,2. | b,,2. | b,,2\upbow r4 | b,,4^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4\< r4 r4 | R2. | }
% c045: 193 Vln I and II marked p at their entry after eight bars' rest (they read mp from 169); violas, cellos and basses are p here (MOLA PDF 8).
